{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "q-331974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "q-331974" "f