ratorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(2)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-10} \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "q-79726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(2)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-8} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-10} \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-8}{c}_{1} \\\\ 4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "q-79726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "26e90ca44d2d42ab0a5e29ef0cb7fefc6039a42ecf3dfb8754d539973eab6da8"} {"question" "q-79726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "q-79726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{4}^{t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "q-79726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3bd85c87bb20bbc4cfa554c6005e1c0631132a5230be41e23af4c110fd8c4c4"} {"question" "e-21120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21120.json" "file_sha256" "4895b8af5fdad2276b6867d89255426f841cc845a14eee04881d6a1e5d72fc39"} {"question" "e-21120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21120.json" "file_sha256" "4895b8af5fdad2276b6867d89255426f841cc845a14eee04881d6a1e5d72fc39"} {"question" "e-21123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21123.json" "file_sha256" "dafd03187ea6868b24a0a20224c85b5f9f37d5bb43a9407205e4b0f4ee20f778"} {"question" "e-21123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/17657d7db880fc0ababc24511039a9d7446952f7521e31ae3351adbb6b28ee61.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/44a286f1a4c15598038f38cdb4140534bac24caacb36f32ee4ef9cbe05b3e5e1.png)\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/d9b9ac7fc20d076110f99476fc48d3c00c4206dc37fb2a929893fcbbb77d6526.png)\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/4f7cfa979200fccf811a7e0d9a633b7919b33b7740c635a921b75ad09995e700.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21123.json" "file_sha256" "dafd03187ea6868b24a0a20224c85b5f9f37d5bb43a9407205e4b0f4ee20f778"} {"question" "e-21124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/5cc29f7492fded69b4100e10329c831cca0734a29966cd1aac2e8ad017636719.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21124.json" "file_sha256" "25fb9e49de65748eb06149215b58c756c108014ad8e5fa320d94c6f62357cb2b"} {"question" "e-21124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)})}^{t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0).$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{0} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21124.json" "file_sha256" "25fb9e49de65748eb06149215b58c756c108014ad8e5fa320d94c6f62357cb2b"} {"question" "q-39268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "626b795acbe8723d3e8791d7fd20e24f8d38c93b2c73728148e7038147288de2" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "The decoupled equations are x1'=0 and x2'=ln(2)x2. Initial values x1(0)=0 and x2(0)=5 give x1(t)=0 and x2(t)=5e^(ln(2)t)=5·2^t. Original option d (index 3) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option c was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. Stored bmatrix and captured square-fenced aligned notation preserve the same ordered components and scalar factor." "bindings" {"problem" "4de9f5e83a1f8e3bba04bb4a32110351509a24c25891f00c4bc5be152c6ed04c" "worked_solution" "fc8318dc210d77c65e6969fffa3eead9cb65e83beb3bb3ef598242e27d60bccf" "original_field" "fa1dbcb8154e5ea1425742550d5b229e5c25c140b226e4eceb458fc41cd3c4d2" "reviewed_field" "0e87ead7596fc7cf7c90c3d99807674bf9b80a3c2bfd68cc560029495dd7f54b" "raw_widget" "30a1b2e4291a2f4b830407dc835e31bb2d65099b1620e6dff85aebaf5524b358"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-39268-before.json" "sha256" "3cd4bf481536f92410682848e8b28fdee61d8af5279bfbf134b4dd7c942d1a54"} {"path" "q-39268-before.png" "sha256" "9364d787a8f0ba6a0d45ca20dd50e4530560b658ff61c9400615acb76d48c868"} {"path" "q-39268-after.png" "sha256" "9bb12522af865e6db36bdd1f97c4de792242a76cd057149c09e2ac87d0fb40e2"} {"path" "history-q-39268.json" "sha256" "8037cc105784ddab2795e4c24ba68e84ad03ab01b48ad1337c0b7e140b91b2fb"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "373bd48d366c1c1f60aa8dbb335e3b97d160fd3d135c299c8490b06fdc78119a"}]} {"question" "q-39268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "626b795acbe8723d3e8791d7fd20e24f8d38c93b2c73728148e7038147288de2" "rationale" "The decoupled equations are x1'=0 and x2'=ln(2)x2. Initial values x1(0)=0 and x2(0)=5 give x1(t)=0 and x2(t)=5e^(ln(2)t)=5·2^t. Original option d (index 3) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option c was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. Stored bmatrix and captured square-fenced aligned notation preserve the same ordered components and scalar factor."}] "usage" {"q-39268" [] "e-21123" [] "e-21120" [] "q-321897" [] "e-21124" [] "q-321901" []} "basis" 2219 "retractions" [[17592186167096 :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = πx_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$"] [17592186167095 :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n2. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n3. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$"] [17592186167254 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\ln (2)\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186167254 :question/answer-fields 17592186165073] [17592186166437 :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 6x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 7x_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$"] [17592186166437 :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$.\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t)$.\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186166438 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t)$."] [17592186166438 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe formula for the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186166439 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\ln (3)\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186166439 :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{1t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2\\ln (3)t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(e^{\\ln (9)})^{t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0)$. Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{0} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163961 17592186163962 17592186163963 17592186163964 17592186163965 17592186163967 17592186163968 17592186163969 17592186163970 17592186163971 17592186165074 17592186165075 17592186165076 17592186165077 17592186165078] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163960 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163960 :answer-field/id] [17592186163960 :answer-field/key] [17592186163960 :answer-field/type] [17592186163966 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163966 :answer-field/id] [17592186163966 :answer-field/key] [17592186163966 :answer-field/type] [17592186165073 :answer-field/correct] [17592186165073 :answer-field/id] [17592186165073 :answer-field/key] [17592186165073 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
